  "activity": {
    "id": "act_retrograde_motion_intro",
    "name": "Apparent Retrograde Motion of Mars",
    "description": "A linear AR activity for introductory astronomy students that first shows Mars tracing a retrograde loop across the night sky, then explains the same event in heliocentric terms as Earth overtakes Mars, and finally compares Mars with Jupiter and Saturn.",
    "workplace": "wp_astronomy_retrograde",
    "start": "step_1_intro",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step_1_intro",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Step 1: Begin the Retrograde Puzzle",
          "description": "Detect the night sky star chart to begin. You will first examine how Mars appears to move against the background stars over many weeks."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "thing_star_chart"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_2_sky_loop",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Step 2: Observe Mars in the Sky",
          "description": "Watch Mars drift eastward, slow down, reverse westward for a loop, and then resume eastward motion. This is the apparent retrograde path seen from Earth\u2019s surface."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_3_geocentric"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_3_geocentric",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Step 3: Compare with the Geocentric Idea",
          "description": "In the geocentric model, Mars\u2019s loop had to be explained with added circular motions called epicycles. This could match the observation, but the explanation became complicated."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "place_solar_view"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_4_heliocentric",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Step 4: View the Heliocentric Explanation",
          "description": "Now switch to an outside view of the solar system. Earth moves on a smaller, faster orbit and periodically overtakes slower Mars. During that pass, Mars appears to move backward against the stars."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_5_prediction"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_5_prediction",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Step 5: Predict When Retrograde Happens",
          "description": "Retrograde for an outer planet occurs near the time Earth catches up to and passes that planet, when the planet is near opposition in the sky. Use this orbital alignment idea to predict when looping should occur."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "thing_comparison_card"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_6_compare_outer_planets",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Step 6: Compare Mars, Jupiter, and Saturn",
          "description": "Compare retrograde loops for three outer planets. Mars usually shows the largest loop, while Jupiter and Saturn produce smaller loops because their greater distances make the apparent shift smaller during Earth\u2019s pass."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_7_summary"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_7_summary",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Step 7: Summarize the Explanation",
          "description": "Mars does not physically reverse its orbit. The loop is an apparent effect produced by changing lines of sight as faster Earth overtakes a slower outer planet. This simpler explanation is why the heliocentric model is preferred."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
